   379	
   380	## 4. Serious Alternative Considered and Rejected
   381	
   382	**Rejected alternative: a global-FIFO replay with an event-driven scene state machine and coverage-driven top-up.** In this design, E3/E4 episodes run until a state-triggered predicate fires (for example, release once `|q̇| < ε` has held for K steps, which mimics "load held"). A coverage monitor detects starved cells and schedules extra episodes or rejects episodes without qualifying windows. The rule controller fills a FIFO rule partition with full episodes. This is the closest patch to the current V3 code, and it would plausibly drive the two marginal counters above zero. I reject it for four reasons.
   383	
   384	1. **It breaks the B01 causal contract.** A release triggered by robot state is an endogenous generator state. `D-B01-001` explicitly classifies state-triggered release progress counters as endogenous, so they can't be passed to `F` as exogenous `ξ_t`. The Group 18 timers would then have to be masked, which reintroduces the E4 phase aliasing that B01 was built to remove. A time-triggered release with jitter gives the same coverage with a fully exogenous `ξ_t`.
   385	2. **A FIFO can't provide a per-cell guarantee at this capacity.** With T = 64, the policy partition holds 1,024 episodes. Under i.i.d. scene/config draws at the nominal quotas, the expected number of resident episodes per cell is λ = 1.42 (E0/E2), 1.07 (E1/E3) and 2.13 (E4). The expected number of empty cells is `144 · Σ_s e^{−λ_s}` ≈ **185.6 of 720**. A 200-draw simulation gave mean 185.5 and range 161–207. These are arithmetic checks, not rollouts. A deterministic evenly spaced schedule rescues the FIFO only barely: the worst cell spacing is 960 episodes (E1/E3) against a 1,024-episode window, a slack of 6.25 % that any invalid-episode re-collection or top-up burst destroys. The rule partition can't hold full episodes for 720 cells at all, since 512 < 720 (`C-B02-A-01`).
   386	3. **Rejection on rollout outcomes biases the data.** Discarding episodes whose policy behaviour failed to produce a window (for example, a policy that never reached `|q̇| < ε`, so the release never fired) removes exactly the failure transitions the critic most needs. It also makes the coverage counts depend on policy competence, which conflates the coverage gate with the competence gate.
   387	4. **Coverage-driven top-up makes counts path-dependent and hard to audit.** Two runs with the same seed but different update timing would hold different cell mixtures. Under the proposed design, `cross_coverage_144_x_5` is a deterministic function of the scheduler pointer.
   388	
